  "como_foi_feito": {
    "nota": "469 of these were composed word by word from a Portuguese-English dictionary; 121 were written by hand. The split is the finding, not an implementation detail.",
    "porque_a_mao": "Composition produces `why_existe_este_file` for `porque_existe_este_ficheiro` and `not_and_aconselhamento_juridico` for `nao_e_aconselhamento_juridico`. 120 keys here are phrases rather than compounds — `o_que_nao_se_pode_verificar`, `o_que_o_portao_novo_encontrou_que_a_revisao_nao_viu` — and a half-translated key ships as a mixture of two languages, which is worse than either. A rule that gets those wrong should not be trusted with the other 469 unchecked either, so every one was read.",
    "encontrado_por": "Running the migration against a scratch clone of this repository and reading what broke. Five distinct classes of defect came out of it, each of which would have reached the live site: folder renames applied child-before-parent leaving an empty folder behind; a pattern that matched `\"dados\"` but not `\"dados/historias.json\"` nor `\"fontes/congeladas\"`; a key map built from dados/ alone, missing every key that lives only in artigos/; the 120 half-translations; and `redacao/` being a READER URL whose backend subfolders move while the page does not."
  },
  "falta_alem_do_mapa": {
    "nota": "This map renames quoted names. It does NOT rename Python identifiers, and that is a gap with teeth rather than a limitation to note in passing.",
    "o_problema": "build/comentarios.py calls junta(quando=..., agente=..., especie=..., porque=...) — Portuguese keyword arguments that build a dict whose keys this map renames. Rename the strings and the code stops agreeing with itself: the function is handed `agente` and reads `agent`. The scratch run died on exactly that, after everything else was already right.",
    "quantos_identificadores": 126,
    "identificadores": [
      "afirmacoes",
      "agente",
      "agente_verbatim",
      "agentes",
      "alvos",
      "arestas",
      "assunto",
      "bancadas",
      "bloco",
      "blocos",
      "bloqueado",
      "bloqueio",
      "caixa",
      "caminho",
      "campo",
      "campos",
      "carga",
      "cartoes",
      "chave",
      "chaves",
      "classe",
      "coluna",
      "colunas",
      "comentarios",
      "consultas",
      "contagem",
      "contagens",
      "cor",
      "corpo",
      "correio",
      "correspondeu",
      "dados",
      "data",
      "datas",
      "decisao",
      "destino",
      "dia",
      "direitos",
      "entidades",
      "entrada"
    ],
    "o_que_e_preciso": "An identifier rename is not a regular expression over quoted strings: it needs to know a name from a string that happens to look like one. Either do it per file by hand, or drive it from Python's own parser. It is the largest remaining piece and it is code-only — no data moves with it."
  },
  "defeitos_encontrados_ao_ensaiar": [
    "Folder renames applied child-before-parent created the target folder, so the parent move was skipped and an empty fontes/ was left behind.",
    "The pattern matched \"dados\" but not \"dados/historias.json\" nor \"fontes/congeladas\" — and the second of those is how nearly every builder refers to a folder.",
    "The key map was built from dados/**/*.json alone and missed every key that lives only in artigos/**/*.json, so the code was renamed and the data was not.",
    "120 keys are phrases rather than compounds and came out half-translated — why_existe_este_file. Each was then written by hand.",
    "redacao/ is a READER URL: redacao/index.html is the newsroom-floor page. Its backend subfolders move; the page does not.",
    "Portuguese Python identifiers — keyword arguments and locals — are invisible to a string-level rename, so code and data drift apart. Unsolved; see falta_alem_do_mapa."
  ]
}
